foundation.solution_text:
$T(0)=15+55e^{0}$
$=70$
As $t\to\infty$, $55e^{-0.3t}\to 0$, so $T\to 15$ from above and $T=15$ is a horizontal asymptote.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$t$}, ylabel={$T$},
            xmin=-0.7, xmax=12.6, ymin=-7, ymax=84, samples=100, clip=false,
            ytick={15,70}, xtick={4,8,12}]
\addplot[dashed, domain=0:12.6] (x, 15);
\addplot[smooth, thick, ->, domain=0:12] {15 + 55*exp(-0.3*x)};
\node[below right] at (axis cs:7,15) {$T=15$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.solution_text:
$N(0)=500+200e^{0}$
$=700$
Since $k=0.15>0$, the term $200e^{0.15t}$ grows, so the curve rises away from the line $N=500$ and there is no limiting value.
